, "cc3": "Med MKT", "cc4": "", "dept_key": "media_marketing", "sub_department": "Unassigned", "cost_centre_label": "Media Marketing", "basis": "team_rule_extended", "confidence": "high"}, {"co": "BEV", "cc3": "Med MKT", "cc4": "DIG MKT", "dept_key": "media_marketing", "sub_department": "Digital Marketing", "cost_centre_label": "Media Marketing", "basis": "team_file_exact", "confidence": "high"}, {"co": "BEV", "cc3": "Med MKT", "cc4": "PPR MED", "dept_key": "media_marketing", "sub_department": "Paper Media", "cost_centre_label": "Media Marketing", "basis": "team_file_exact", "confidence": "high"}, {"co": "BEV", "cc3": "OTE", "cc4": "", "dept_key": "one_time_expense", "sub_department": "Unassigned", "cost_centre_label": "One Time Expense", "basis": "inferred_new", "confidence": "low"}, {"co": "BEV", "cc3": "OTE", "cc4": "(blank)", "dept_key": "one_time_expense", "sub_department": "Unassigned", "cost_centre_label": "One Time Expense", "basis": "inferred_new", "confidence": "low"}, {"co": "BEV", "cc3": "Sales", "cc4": "CSD", "dept_key": "sales", "sub_department": "Canteen Stores Dept", "cost_centre_label": "Sales", "basis": "team_file_exact", "confidence": "high"}, {"co": "BEV", "cc3": "Sales", "cc4": "GT", "dept_key": "sales", "sub_department": "General Trade", "cost_centre_label": "Sales", "basis": "team_file_exact", "confidence": "high"}], "notes": "COVERAGE (verified, not asserted). sap-combos.csv = 97 lines, 96 data rows (OIL 49, MART 25, BEV 22). My mapping array is 96 rows and its ordered (CO, OCRCODE3, OCRCODE4) list is byte-identical to the CSV's \u2014 checked in code, plus a Counter duplicate scan that found zero repeated keys. No agent dropped or duplicated a combo either: Oil returned 49, Mart 25, Bev 22, and each set-differenced empty against its company's CSV rows. Net total of all 96 rows = INR 21,10,56,446 (Rs 21.11 Cr). Amounts came only from the CSV; I did not carry them into the output because the schema has no amount field, but every department total below was computed from the CSV rows, not from the agents' restated figures.\n\nRULE CONFIRMED FROM THE FILE, NOT FROM THE BRIEF. I grouped team-dept-map.csv (44 data rows) by OcrCode3 and counted distinct departments per Dim3. Exactly three Dim3 values fan out: BackOff {Accounts, Hr Admin, Legal}, Sales {Sales, Sales E-Com}, Sales RE {Sales, Sales E-Com}. Every other Dim3 collapses to one department across all its Dim4s, including FACT_COM and Factory, which are constant at Plant & Production across Dim4 = Accounts/GT/PLANT/CIVIL/GP-GDWN/(blank) \u2014 that is the proof Dim4 is ignored under the Factory family, not an assumption. So: Dim4 decides for BackOff / Sales / Sales RE, Dim3 decides everywhere else. NPD1, NPD2 and OTE are present in the team file with an EMPTY department \u2014 the team has seen them and chose not to classify them.\n\nCONFLICTS BETWEEN THE THREE AGENTS, AND RESOLUTIONS.\n1. OTE (One Time Expense) \u2014 the only real three-way split. Oil said a standalone \"One Time Expense\" department; Mart and Bev both said Accounts. I did NOT follow the 2-to-1 majority. Oil's rows are -43.08 L on 11 txns against +25.96 L on 29 txns (net -17.12 L), and Bev's are an exact -4,250/+4,250 pair: that is reclassification traffic, not spend. Folding it into Accounts would put a negative reclassification bucket inside a real department's expense line and make Accounts read wrong. Resolved as its own department, ordered 10th, immediately before Short & Excess. All 5 OTE rows are inferred_new / low. This is the one place Accounts should be asked to rule before the number is published.\n2. NPD1/NPD2 \u2014 Oil and Mart both invented \"New Product Development\"; Bev had no NPD rows. Adopted as written. But note it is inferred_new (medium), not the team's word: the team left it blank on purpose, so this department is my construction and the alternative homes (Plant & Production for factory trials, Media Marketing for launch spend) are live options. Weight is material \u2014 INR 1.22 Cr across 6 rows \u2014 which is why I put it at order 9 with the pending/unclassified tail rather than at its money rank (it would otherwise sit above Media Marketing and Legal).\n3. sub_department naming \u2014 the same Dim4 got different labels from different agents: FACT_COM/Accounts was \"Factory Common - Accounts\" (Oil) vs \"Accounts\" (Bev), FACT_COM/GT was \"Factory Common - General Trade\" vs \"General Trade\", and Bev used em-dashes where Oil used hyphens. Resolved by rule: sub_department is the readable Dim4 name ALONE, because cost_centre_label already carries the Dim3. So \"Accounts\", \"General Trade\", \"Unassigned\" \u2014 never a Dim3 prefix, never an em-dash. Also standardised Med MKT/POP from Oil's \"Paper Media (POP)\" to \"Paper Media\", matching Bev's PPR MED.\n4. Blank Dim4 under a Dim4-decides Dim3 \u2014 the rule genuinely returns no answer here, and the two agents that met it chose differently in spirit. MART/BackOff/(blank): Mart said Hr Admin; kept, because 4 of BackOff's 7 Dim4s are Hr Admin, but marked inferred_new / low (INR 0 on 1 txn, immaterial either way). MART/Sales RE with no Dim4 (INR 7.11 L + 2.15 L): kept Mart's Sales, since Sales is the default for every non-E-COM Dim4 in that family \u2014 but flagged medium, because Mart's Sales RE is overwhelmingly E-COM by money (INR 3.52 Cr), so these blanks may really belong to Sales E-Com.\n5. basis inflation, corrected against all three agents. Rows whose raw Dim4 is truly null (\"\") match the team file only through its literal \"(blank)\" twin. Oil and Bev honestly called those team_rule_extended; a naive normalisation would call them exact. I applied the honest label across all three companies: 12 rows moved from team_file_exact to team_rule_extended (Del Bkhp, Del Mayp's family, FACT_COM, Factory, Transprt, Sal CF, Med MKT and the blank-Dim3 rows). Final basis counts: 57 team_file_exact, 17 team_rule_extended, 22 inferred_new. Confidence: 72 high, 15 medium, 9 low.\n6. Mart-only Dim3 values, which appear nowhere in the team file and therefore have no team ruling at all: ECOM -> Sales E-Com (high, INR 1.83 Cr, unambiguous by function), HR/ADMIN -> Hr Admin (high), SERVICES -> Hr Admin (LOW \u2014 Mart's own agent flagged it and I agree; INR 5.59 L over 147 txns could equally be professional fees under Accounts/Legal or third-party e-com fulfilment under Sales E-Com; the top-10 vendors on that cost centre would settle it), SUPPLY-C -> Transportation (SC-BHKR medium because it mirrors Oil's Del Bkhp/Bhakharpur; SC-WARH low because warehouse operations may belong under Plant & Production \u2014 INR 18.46 L at stake).\n7. Truly-null vs literal \"(blank)\" Dim3. All three agents put both forms under Short & Excess and I kept that, but I lowered the null-Dim3 rows to medium and I am flagging the one that matters: MART with no Dim3 at all is INR -94.14 L over 30 txns, and BEV's is +16.94 L over just 4 txns. Large, few, wholly un-dimensioned postings look like journal/adjustment traffic that was never cost-centre tagged, not short-and-excess. The team file only ever mapped the literal (blank)/(blank) pair. Recommend Accounts confirm whether these should be re-tagged rather than reported as Short & Excess.\n\nTYPO AND NAMING. \"Transpotation\" -> \"Transportation\" (the only spelling fix; label and slug both). Everything else keeps the team's own wording, including \"Hr Admin\" (not \"HR Admin\") and \"Short & Excess\". \"Sales E-Com\" stays a separate department from \"Sales\" because the team keeps it separate in both Sales and Sales RE.\n\nORDER RATIONALE. Money computed from the CSV: Sales E-Com 7.71 Cr, Transportation 3.37 Cr, Plant & Production 3.30 Cr, Sales 2.34 Cr, Accounts 2.09 Cr, Hr Admin 1.81 Cr, New Product Development 1.22 Cr, Media Marketing 41.3 L, Legal 20.96 L, One Time Expense -17.11 L, Short & Excess -1.19 Cr. Order is money-led with two deliberate departures: the two sales lines are kept adjacent (1-2) so the split reads as one commercial block, and Plant & Production is placed ahead of the marginally larger Transportation (a 2% gap) because production is the more operationally central function and spans 18 cost-centre combos to Transportation's 11. The three unclassified/catch-all lines \u2014 New Product Development, One Time Expense, Short & Excess \u2014 are last regardless of money."}